## Scope — read this before wiring it up

The tree **structure** and **Sequence/Selector evaluation** are real and
deterministic — this walks your authored node graph exactly as specified.
The **leaves are extension hooks, not autonomous AI**:

- **Action** nodes call `SendMessage(action, SendMessageOptions.DontRequireReceiver)`
  on this GameObject. `SendMessage` has no return value, so an Action node
  always reports `Success` to its parent — attach a script with a method
  matching the action name to actually do something.
- **Condition** nodes look up a `Func<bool>` registered via
  `RegisterCondition(name, fn)`. An unregistered condition logs a warning and
  defaults to `true` (deterministic, doesn't crash the tree, but visibly flags
  the gap) — call `RegisterCondition` from `Start()` on another script to wire
  real decision logic.
- **Decorator** nodes are a minimal pass-through to their first child —
  invert/repeat/cooldown semantics aren't modeled generically.

## Install

1. Drop the `Scripts/` folder into your project's `Assets/`.
2. Add the `{{ s.name }}` component to the AI GameObject.
3. Wire real behavior from another script:

```csharp
var bt = GetComponent<{{ s.name }}>();
bt.RegisterCondition("PlayerInRange", () => Vector3.Distance(transform.position, player.position) < 10f);
// Action nodes fire SendMessage — add methods matching each action name:
void ChasePlayer() { /* your movement logic */ }
```

